From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 13:43:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): fuse the MoE weighted reduction
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

build_moe_ffn ends each MoE layer with MUL(experts, weights), one VIEW per
used expert and an ADD chain ((v0 + v1) + v2) + ... On the CPU each MUL
and ADD is a separate pass over [n_embd, n_expert_used, n_tokens] floats
with a full thread-pool barrier after it, and the MUL with a [1, k, T]
operand runs its broadcast one element at a time.

ggml_cpu_try_fuse_ops now matches this pattern, in both the
experts * weights and the (experts * expert_scale) * weights forms, and
runs one kernel that writes the last ADD's tensor. The kernel splits the
work over (token, block of 64 columns), so one decode token still spreads
over the threads. It replaces the k MUL and ADD barriers (k + 1 with the
expert scale) with one, or two when it stages the weights, and it removes
the weighted [n_embd, k, T] intermediate.

The result is bit-exact with the unfused nodes. Each product is rounded
as the MUL rounds it, and the sum runs in expert order as the ADD chain
does. The products pass through a stack buffer behind a compiler barrier,
so GCC (-ffp-contract=fast by default) cannot contract a product and its
add into an FMA. MSVC has no such barrier here, and its default
/fp:precise does not contract.

The allocator can place the last ADD's buffer over the weights tensor,
because the unfused MUL is its last consumer. On Qwen3.8-Flash-Next this
happens in the prompt graphs. CUDA prevents this in graph_optimize with
add_alloc_dep. The CPU backend has no graph_optimize hook, and callers
outside ggml_backend_sched would not get one, so the kernel copies the
weights and the expert scale to the work buffer behind one barrier before
any thread writes dst. ggml_graph_plan reserves 2 floats per row for a
MUL whose src1 has the [1, k, T] shape of the weights, with 2 <= k <= 15.
The fusion is skipped when the buffer overlaps the experts.

The matcher is a C port of ggml_cuda_match_moe_weighted_reduction
(ggml-cuda.cu). ik_llama.cpp has the same fusion as GGML_OP_MUL_MULTI_ADD,
added in ik_llama.cpp PR #858 ("Fused mul + multi_add op"). test_moe_reduce
gets k = 10 cases at n_embd 2560 for 1, 4 and 512 tokens.

On perf/stack2 the fusion meets the serial runs of tiny ops. A MUL that
fits GGML_CPU_SERIAL_BYTES and starts the pattern runs the fused kernel on
thread 0 alone. The other threads then walk the covered MUL and ADD nodes
one by one, so they agree on the barriers only if each covered node is
serial too. ggml_cpu_node_is_serial now gets the fused span from
ggml_cpu_serial_fusion_span and requires every covered node to fit, as it
already did for RMS_NORM + MUL. With nth = 1 the kernel skips its staging
barrier, because the other threads wait at the barrier after the run. The
matcher uses ggml_cpu_ranges_overlap from the gated_delta_net fusion in
place of a second copy.

